            "processed": true,
            "summary": "This document is a case report detailing two incidents involving adverse reactions to the medication Neptazol, prescribed for hypertension. \n\nIn Case 1, a 52-year-old woman experienced a generalized urticarial rash and mild lip swelling after ten days of taking Neptazol. She received outpatient treatment with antihistamines, and her symptoms resolved within 48 hours after discontinuing the medication.\n\nIn Case 2, a 67-year-old man experienced more severe symptoms, including facial angioedema and difficulty swallowing, after three weeks of Neptazol use. He required emergency treatment with intramuscular epinephrine and intravenous corticosteroids and was monitored overnight before being discharged from the hospital. Neptazol was permanently discontinued for him, and he was advised to avoid the drug class in the future.\n\nThis document is relevant to patient-safety and quality complaints because it outlines serious adverse reactions associated with Neptazol, including one case that required emergency medical intervention. The information may be useful for assessing the safety profile of this medication and guiding future use and reporting in similar cases."
        }
    ],
